1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,360
Spring, 146 BC.

2
00:00:03,740 --> 00:00:06,853
On the coast of North Africa, a city is dying.

3
00:00:07,233 --> 00:00:14,929
For six days and six nights, Roman soldiers have been fighting their way up three narrow streets toward the citadel at its heart.

4
00:00:15,309 --> 00:00:18,454
The houses on either side are six storeys tall.

5
00:00:18,834 --> 00:00:23,350
Every one of them has to be taken, floor by floor, roof by roof.

6
00:00:25,350 --> 00:00:28,703
When a block was cleared, the Romans set it on fire.

7
00:00:29,083 --> 00:00:35,010
The ancient historian Appian, following an eyewitness account, describes what came next.

8
00:00:35,390 --> 00:00:43,483
Men with axes and hooks cleared the rubble to open a path for the troops, and they swept the dead and the living alike into pits.

9
00:00:43,863 --> 00:00:45,588
Cavalry rode over them.

10
00:00:47,588 --> 00:00:53,112
On the seventh day, a group of men came down from the citadel holding olive branches.

11
00:00:53,492 --> 00:00:57,675
They asked for one thing: that those who came out should be allowed to live.

12
00:00:58,055 --> 00:01:03,322
Fifty thousand men, women and children walked out of the last stronghold of the city.

13
00:01:03,702 --> 00:01:05,579
They would be sold as slaves.

14
00:01:07,579 --> 00:01:15,634
Watching from a high place, the Roman commander, Publius Cornelius Scipio Aemilianus, looked out over the burning city.

15
00:01:16,014 --> 00:01:19,874
Beside him stood his friend, the Greek historian Polybius.

16
00:01:20,254 --> 00:01:23,388
And Polybius saw the conqueror of Carthage weep.

17
00:01:25,388 --> 00:01:26,992
The city was Carthage.

18
00:01:27,372 --> 00:01:32,439
For more than a century it had fought Rome for control of the Mediterranean world.

19
00:01:32,819 --> 00:01:39,556
Its greatest general had marched elephants over the Alps and destroyed Roman armies on Italian soil.

20
00:01:39,936 --> 00:01:42,079
Now it was being wiped from the earth.

21
00:01:42,459 --> 00:01:45,429
This is the story of how that happened, and why.

22
00:02:03,929 --> 00:02:08,591
The story begins far to the east, on the coast of what is now Lebanon.

23
00:02:08,971 --> 00:02:18,602
Here, three thousand years ago, stood the cities of the Phoenicians: Byblos, Sidon, and above all Tyre, built on an island just off the shore.

24
00:02:18,982 --> 00:02:23,001
The Phoenicians were the great sailors and traders of their age.

25
00:02:23,381 --> 00:02:29,659
They sold cedar wood, glass, and a purple dye so costly that it became the colour of kings.

26
00:02:31,659 --> 00:02:35,531
They also spread something even more valuable: their alphabet.

27
00:02:35,911 --> 00:02:41,836
The Greeks borrowed it, and from the Greek alphabet came the Latin letters you are reading on this screen.

28
00:02:42,216 --> 00:02:53,241
Phoenician ships pushed further and further west, past Cyprus, Sicily and Sardinia, all the way through the Strait of Gibraltar, founding trading posts along the way.

29
00:02:55,241 --> 00:03:01,155
One of those settlements, on a peninsula in what is now Tunisia, was given a simple name.

30
00:03:01,535 --> 00:03:02,745
Qart-hadasht.

31
00:03:03,125 --> 00:03:04,143
The New City.

32
00:03:04,523 --> 00:03:06,610
The Greeks called it Karchedon.

33
00:03:06,990 --> 00:03:09,102
The Romans called it Carthago.

34
00:03:09,482 --> 00:03:10,858
We call it Carthage.

35
00:03:12,858 --> 00:03:20,350
According to the legend, the city was founded by a princess of Tyre named Elissa, whom the Romans would call Dido.

36
00:03:20,730 --> 00:03:25,175
Her brother, King Pygmalion, had murdered her husband for his wealth.

37
00:03:25,555 --> 00:03:31,682
Elissa fled with her followers and the treasure, sailed west, and landed on the African shore.

38
00:03:33,682 --> 00:03:39,055
She asked the local ruler for as much land as could be covered by the hide of an ox.

39
00:03:39,435 --> 00:03:40,363
He agreed.

40
00:03:40,743 --> 00:03:46,640
So she cut the hide into strips so thin that, laid end to end, they circled a whole hill.

41
00:03:47,020 --> 00:03:50,551
That hill became the citadel of Carthage, the Byrsa.

42
00:03:50,931 --> 00:03:53,359
In Greek, byrsa means ox-hide.

43
00:03:53,739 --> 00:03:59,284
It is a good story, but it is almost certainly a pun on a Phoenician word for a fortress.

44
00:04:01,284 --> 00:04:03,807
Roman writers added a love story.

45
00:04:04,187 --> 00:04:15,664
In Virgil's Aeneid, Dido falls in love with the Trojan hero Aeneas, and when he abandons her to found Rome she kills herself, cursing his descendants with eternal enmity.

46
00:04:16,044 --> 00:04:25,169
It is pure poetry, written centuries later, but it tells you how the Romans came to see Carthage: as the enemy destined from the beginning.

47
00:04:27,169 --> 00:04:32,306
The ancient tradition dated the founding to 814 BC.

48
00:04:32,686 --> 00:04:35,513
For a long time historians doubted it.

49
00:04:35,893 --> 00:04:45,033
But the oldest objects found in the city date from the late ninth or eighth century BC, close enough to suggest the tradition was not far wrong.

50
00:04:45,413 --> 00:04:48,062
Carthage was older than Rome itself.

51
00:04:59,062 --> 00:05:06,381
When Tyre itself fell under the power of the great empires of the East, Carthage became the leader of the Phoenician West.

52
00:05:06,761 --> 00:05:17,121
By the fourth century BC it controlled the coast of North Africa, western Sicily, Sardinia, the Balearic Islands and stretches of southern Spain.

53
00:05:17,501 --> 00:05:19,894
Its fleets kept rival traders out.

54
00:05:20,274 --> 00:05:26,362
Its merchants bought tin, silver, grain and slaves, and sold them across the Mediterranean.

55
00:05:28,362 --> 00:05:33,162
The city itself grew into one of the largest in the Mediterranean world.

56
00:05:33,542 --> 00:05:42,274
Its houses climbed the slopes of the Byrsa, with tall apartment blocks on narrow streets, and grand villas with gardens on the outskirts.

57
00:05:42,654 --> 00:05:46,774
Water was precious, so almost every house had its own cistern.

58
00:05:47,154 --> 00:05:55,709
Archaeologists have found bathtubs, mosaic floors and drains that would not look out of place in a Roman town centuries later.

59
00:05:57,709 --> 00:06:00,174
Most famous of all were the harbours.

60
00:06:00,554 --> 00:06:06,604
A ship entering from the sea passed first into a long rectangular basin for merchant ships.

61
00:06:06,984 --> 00:06:12,140
A channel led from there into a second, circular harbour reserved for the navy.

62
00:06:12,520 --> 00:06:18,304
In its centre was an island with a tower, from which the admiral could watch the open sea.

63
00:06:18,684 --> 00:06:30,895
All around the edge of the basin, and on the island, were covered sheds for some two hundred and twenty warships, each fronted by a pair of columns, so that the whole harbour looked like one great colonnade.

64
00:06:32,895 --> 00:06:35,326
Carthage was a republic, like Rome.

65
00:06:35,706 --> 00:06:39,543
Each year it elected two chief magistrates, called suffetes.

66
00:06:39,923 --> 00:06:47,702
There was a council of elders and a popular assembly, and a court of one hundred and four judges who watched over the generals.

67
00:06:48,082 --> 00:06:53,184
A general who lost a battle could be put on trial, and sometimes crucified.

68
00:06:53,564 --> 00:07:00,431
The Greek philosopher Aristotle admired the Carthaginian constitution and praised its stability.

69
00:07:02,431 --> 00:07:13,356
Its people worshipped Phoenician gods: Baal Hammon, and a goddess called Tanit, whose sign, a triangle with a circle above it, is carved on thousands of stones.

70
00:07:13,736 --> 00:07:19,382
Some of those stones stand in a sacred precinct that archaeologists call the tophet.

71
00:07:19,762 --> 00:07:25,425
Under them were buried urns holding the cremated bones of infants and young animals.

72
00:07:27,425 --> 00:07:35,498
Greek and Roman writers said that the Carthaginians sacrificed their own children to their gods in times of crisis.

73
00:07:35,878 --> 00:07:44,625
Some modern scholars have argued that this was enemy propaganda, and that the tophet was simply a cemetery for children who died young.

74
00:07:45,005 --> 00:07:51,639
The debate goes on, but many archaeologists now accept that sacrifices did happen, at least at times.

75
00:07:52,019 --> 00:07:58,248
It is worth remembering that almost every word we have about Carthage was written by its enemies.

76
00:08:00,248 --> 00:08:05,416
For centuries Carthage fought the Greek cities of Sicily, above all Syracuse.

77
00:08:05,796 --> 00:08:10,501
With Rome, a small city in central Italy, it was on friendly terms.

78
00:08:10,881 --> 00:08:19,186
The Greek historian Polybius copied out the text of a treaty between the two cities made in the first year of the Roman Republic.

79
00:08:19,566 --> 00:08:22,287
Rome was to stay out of Carthage's waters.

80
00:08:22,667 --> 00:08:26,280
Carthage was to leave Rome's Latin allies alone.

81
00:08:26,660 --> 00:08:29,531
For two hundred and fifty years, it worked.

82
00:08:40,531 --> 00:08:52,791
In 264 BC, a band of mercenaries who had seized the Sicilian city of Messana found themselves under attack, and appealed for help to both Carthage and Rome.

83
00:08:53,171 --> 00:08:55,967
Rome had by now conquered most of Italy.

84
00:08:56,347 --> 00:09:02,496
After a fierce debate, the Romans decided to send an army across the narrow strait into Sicily.

85
00:09:02,876 --> 00:09:06,727
It was the first time a Roman army had ever left Italy.

86
00:09:07,107 --> 00:09:13,182
It began the first of three wars that the Romans called Punic, from their word for Phoenician.

87
00:09:15,182 --> 00:09:17,226
Carthage was a naval power.

88
00:09:17,606 --> 00:09:18,751
Rome was not.

89
00:09:19,131 --> 00:09:25,955
The Romans had almost no warships, and none of the heavy galleys called quinqueremes that dominated the sea.

90
00:09:26,335 --> 00:09:41,355
According to Polybius, they found a Carthaginian quinquereme that had run aground, used it as a model, and built a hundred more in sixty days, training their oarsmen on benches set up on dry land while the ships were being finished.

91
00:09:43,355 --> 00:09:52,442
To make up for their lack of skill, the Romans invented a boarding bridge with a heavy iron spike on the end, which they called the corvus, the raven.

92
00:09:52,822 --> 00:10:02,407
When an enemy ship came close, the bridge dropped, the spike drove into the deck, and Roman soldiers ran across to fight as if they were on land.

93
00:10:02,787 --> 00:10:10,127
At Mylae in 260 BC the Carthaginian fleet was taken completely by surprise.

94
00:10:12,126 --> 00:10:14,645
The war dragged on for twenty-three years.

95
00:10:15,025 --> 00:10:26,815
In 256 BC, after one of the largest sea battles in history, the Romans landed an army in Africa itself under the consul Marcus Atilius Regulus.

96
00:10:27,195 --> 00:10:29,190
Carthage seemed about to fall.

97
00:10:29,570 --> 00:10:39,138
But the Carthaginians hired a Spartan mercenary, Xanthippus, who used their elephants and cavalry to smash the Roman army on open ground.

98
00:10:39,518 --> 00:10:41,223
Regulus was captured.

99
00:10:43,223 --> 00:10:45,767
Then the sea itself turned against Rome.

100
00:10:46,147 --> 00:10:55,711
In 255 BC, a Roman fleet ignored the warnings of its pilots and was caught by a storm off the south coast of Sicily.

101
00:10:56,091 --> 00:11:01,416
According to Polybius, of three hundred and sixty-four ships, only eighty survived.

102
00:11:01,796 --> 00:11:04,016
Tens of thousands of men drowned.

103
00:11:04,396 --> 00:11:07,720
Even so, the Romans simply built another fleet.

104
00:11:09,720 --> 00:11:11,949
In Sicily the war ground on.

105
00:11:12,329 --> 00:11:28,021
A Carthaginian general named Hamilcar, nicknamed Barca, probably meaning lightning, held out for years on a mountain near Panormus, modern Palermo, and later at Eryx, raiding the Italian coast and never losing a battle.

106
00:11:28,401 --> 00:11:29,922
Remember his name.

107
00:11:30,302 --> 00:11:32,825
His family would shape the next fifty years.

108
00:11:34,825 --> 00:11:45,569
In 241 BC, with its treasury empty, the Roman Senate accepted loans from private citizens to build one last fleet of two hundred ships.

109
00:11:45,949 --> 00:11:54,071
Off the Aegates Islands, west of Sicily, it caught a Carthaginian supply fleet heavily loaded and destroyed it.

110
00:11:54,451 --> 00:12:05,823
Since 2004, divers have found more than twenty bronze warship rams from this battle on the seabed, some still inscribed with the names of the officials who commissioned them.

111
00:12:07,823 --> 00:12:09,605
Carthage asked for peace.

112
00:12:09,985 --> 00:12:20,889
It gave up Sicily, which became Rome's first overseas province, and agreed to pay a huge indemnity of three thousand two hundred talents of silver over ten years.

113
00:12:21,269 --> 00:12:27,710
Hamilcar came down from his mountain undefeated, and never forgave the men who had made him surrender.

114
00:12:38,710 --> 00:12:40,783
Peace brought Carthage no rest.

115
00:12:41,163 --> 00:12:48,070
Its army in Sicily had been made up largely of mercenaries: Libyans, Iberians, Gauls, Greeks.

116
00:12:48,450 --> 00:12:53,043
Twenty thousand of them were shipped back to Africa expecting to be paid.

117
00:12:53,423 --> 00:12:55,167
The treasury was empty.

118
00:12:55,547 --> 00:13:01,799
The mercenaries mutinied, and the Libyan towns that Carthage had taxed so heavily rose with them.

119
00:13:03,799 --> 00:13:08,222
What followed was so savage that Polybius called it the Truceless War.

120
00:13:08,602 --> 00:13:14,163
The rebels tortured and killed a Carthaginian envoy and seven hundred prisoners.

121
00:13:14,543 --> 00:13:16,594
Carthage answered in kind.

122
00:13:16,974 --> 00:13:31,982
Hamilcar Barca was given command, and after three years he trapped the main rebel army in a mountain valley known as the Saw, where the starving men, Polybius says, ate their prisoners and then their slaves before they surrendered.

123
00:13:33,982 --> 00:13:36,479
The revolt nearly destroyed Carthage.

124
00:13:36,859 --> 00:13:39,293
And while it was helpless, Rome struck.

125
00:13:39,673 --> 00:13:51,214
In 238 BC, the Romans seized the island of Sardinia, and when Carthage protested, threatened war again unless it paid a further twelve hundred talents.

126
00:13:51,594 --> 00:13:56,967
Polybius, who admired Rome, admitted that this was without any justification.

127
00:13:57,347 --> 00:14:01,515
Many Carthaginians, Hamilcar among them, never forgot it.

128
00:14:03,515 --> 00:14:12,712
In 237 BC, Hamilcar sailed for Spain with an army, to build Carthage a new empire there, rich in silver.

129
00:14:13,092 --> 00:14:16,196
Before he left, he made a sacrifice to the gods.

130
00:14:16,576 --> 00:14:19,585
His nine-year-old son begged to come with him.

131
00:14:19,965 --> 00:14:22,627
Hamilcar agreed, on one condition.

132
00:14:23,007 --> 00:14:30,274
He led the boy to the altar, made him place his hand on the sacrifice, and swear that he would never be a friend to Rome.

133
00:14:30,654 --> 00:14:32,574
The boy's name was Hannibal.

134
00:14:34,574 --> 00:14:40,531
We know this story because Hannibal told it himself, many years later, to a foreign king.

135
00:14:40,911 --> 00:14:43,879
Whether every detail is true, we cannot say.

136
00:14:44,259 --> 00:14:46,778
But it is how he wanted to be understood.

137
00:14:48,778 --> 00:14:55,313
In Spain, Hamilcar conquered and made alliances, and took over the rich silver mines of the south.

138
00:14:55,693 --> 00:15:04,048
When he drowned in a river during a battle in 229 BC, his son-in-law, Hasdrubal the Fair, took over.

139
00:15:04,428 --> 00:15:10,297
Hasdrubal founded a new capital on a superb natural harbour on the Spanish coast.

140
00:15:10,677 --> 00:15:12,984
He called it, too, Qart-hadasht.

141
00:15:13,364 --> 00:15:15,563
The Romans called it New Carthage.

142
00:15:15,943 --> 00:15:19,048
Today it is the Spanish city of Cartagena.

143
00:15:21,048 --> 00:15:29,408
Rome, worried about this new power, made a treaty with Hasdrubal: the Carthaginians would not cross the river Ebro in arms.

144
00:15:29,788 --> 00:15:36,085
Then, in 221 BC, Hasdrubal was murdered by a Celt seeking revenge.

145
00:15:36,465 --> 00:15:40,320
The army in Spain chose a new commander by acclamation.

146
00:15:40,700 --> 00:15:42,599
He was twenty-six years old.

147
00:15:42,979 --> 00:15:44,591
It was Hamilcar's son.

148
00:15:44,971 --> 00:15:45,702
Hannibal.

149
00:15:56,702 --> 00:15:59,154
We do not know what Hannibal looked like.

150
00:15:59,534 --> 00:16:03,032
No portrait of him survives that we can be sure of.

151
00:16:03,412 --> 00:16:14,532
Some silver coins minted by his family in Spain show a young beardless man and an older bearded one, and some historians think these are Barca family portraits.

152
00:16:14,912 --> 00:16:20,604
A marble bust in Naples has been called Hannibal since the Renaissance, but it is a guess.

153
00:16:22,604 --> 00:16:26,132
What we do have are descriptions written by his enemies.

154
00:16:26,512 --> 00:16:34,362
The Roman historian Livy, writing two centuries later, says that no man was ever better at both obeying and commanding.

155
00:16:34,742 --> 00:16:37,178
He endured heat and cold alike.

156
00:16:37,558 --> 00:16:41,113
He ate and slept when there was time, not when it was night.

157
00:16:41,493 --> 00:16:47,422
He was often seen asleep on the ground among the sentries, wrapped in a common soldier's cloak.

158
00:16:47,802 --> 00:16:51,046
He was the first into battle and the last to leave it.

159
00:16:53,046 --> 00:16:59,056
Livy also gave him a list of vices: inhuman cruelty, and more than Punic treachery.

160
00:16:59,436 --> 00:17:03,485
That phrase, Punic faith, became a Roman proverb for lying.

161
00:17:03,865 --> 00:17:06,020
We should remember who was writing.

162
00:17:08,020 --> 00:17:11,786
Hannibal spent two years strengthening his hold on Spain.

163
00:17:12,166 --> 00:17:20,300
Then he turned to Saguntum, a wealthy town on the coast south of the Ebro, which had placed itself under Rome's protection.

164
00:17:20,680 --> 00:17:24,337
In 219 BC he laid siege to it.

165
00:17:24,717 --> 00:17:30,124
The Saguntines held out for eight months, expecting Roman help that never came.

166
00:17:30,504 --> 00:17:37,785
When the city fell, many of its people, according to Livy, burned themselves and their possessions rather than surrender.

167
00:17:39,785 --> 00:17:44,289
In Rome, the news caused outrage, and an embassy sailed to Carthage.

168
00:17:44,669 --> 00:17:52,128
Its leader, Quintus Fabius, stood before the Carthaginian council and gathered a fold of his toga in his hand.

169
00:17:52,508 --> 00:17:55,199
Here, he said, we bring you war and peace.

170
00:17:55,579 --> 00:17:57,285
Take whichever you please.

171
00:17:57,665 --> 00:18:01,422
Give us whichever you like, the Carthaginians shouted back.

172
00:18:01,802 --> 00:18:05,048
Then I give you war, he said, and let the fold fall.

173
00:18:05,428 --> 00:18:07,590
They answered that they accepted it.

174
00:18:09,590 --> 00:18:12,657
Rome expected to fight the war on its own terms.

175
00:18:13,037 --> 00:18:14,807
Its fleet ruled the sea.

176
00:18:15,187 --> 00:18:20,626
One consul, Publius Cornelius Scipio, would sail to Spain to deal with Hannibal.

177
00:18:21,006 --> 00:18:23,057
The other would invade Africa.

178
00:18:23,438 --> 00:18:25,609
But Hannibal had a different plan.

179
00:18:25,989 --> 00:18:38,509
If he could not reach Italy by sea, he would go by land, across the Pyrenees, across the Rhône, and over the Alps, and fight the Romans in their own country, where their allies might abandon them.

180
00:18:49,509 --> 00:18:54,928
In the late spring of 218 BC, Hannibal marched out of New Carthage.

181
00:18:55,308 --> 00:19:01,966
Polybius gives him ninety thousand infantry, twelve thousand cavalry, and thirty-seven war elephants.

182
00:19:02,346 --> 00:19:06,711
The starting numbers may be exaggerated, but it was a huge force.

183
00:19:07,091 --> 00:19:15,740
He crossed the Ebro, fought his way through the tribes north of it, and climbed over the Pyrenees, leaving troops behind to hold what he had won.

184
00:19:17,740 --> 00:19:21,633
At the Rhône, a Gallic tribe waited on the far bank to stop him.

185
00:19:22,013 --> 00:19:30,899
Hannibal sent part of his army upstream by night, under an officer named Hanno, to cross in secret and fall on the Gauls from behind.

186
00:19:31,279 --> 00:19:33,076
The elephants were a problem.

187
00:19:33,456 --> 00:19:35,724
They refused to step onto boats.

188
00:19:36,104 --> 00:19:46,526
So the Carthaginians built huge rafts, covered them with earth so that they looked like solid ground, and coaxed the animals onto them with two females leading the way.

189
00:19:48,526 --> 00:19:55,337
Days later, a Roman army under the consul Scipio landed at the mouth of the Rhône, on its way to Spain.

190
00:19:55,717 --> 00:20:00,904
It arrived to find that Hannibal had already gone, three days earlier, heading inland.

191
00:20:01,284 --> 00:20:03,584
Scipio made a remarkable decision.

192
00:20:03,964 --> 00:20:11,867
He sent his army on to Spain under his brother, to cut Hannibal off from his base, and sailed back to Italy alone to meet him there.

193
00:20:13,867 --> 00:20:16,856
It was autumn when Hannibal reached the mountains.

194
00:20:17,236 --> 00:20:24,347
Mountain tribes attacked his long column in narrow passes, rolling rocks down onto men and pack animals.

195
00:20:24,728 --> 00:20:27,773
Horses and mules slipped and fell from the paths.

196
00:20:28,153 --> 00:20:31,315
At the top of the pass, snow was already falling.

197
00:20:31,695 --> 00:20:39,547
Polybius says that Hannibal gathered his exhausted men and pointed out the plains of Italy, far below, to give them heart.

198
00:20:41,547 --> 00:20:43,343
The way down was worse.

199
00:20:43,723 --> 00:20:46,204
A landslide had swept away the path.

200
00:20:46,584 --> 00:20:52,548
Livy says the soldiers split a great rock by heating it with fire and pouring vinegar on it.

201
00:20:52,928 --> 00:20:57,259
Polybius, who walked the route himself to check it, does not mention this.

202
00:20:57,639 --> 00:21:00,514
The whole crossing took about fifteen days.

203
00:21:02,514 --> 00:21:04,121
Which pass did he use?

204
00:21:04,501 --> 00:21:06,193
Nobody knows for certain.

205
00:21:06,573 --> 00:21:10,015
Historians have argued for at least half a dozen routes.

206
00:21:10,395 --> 00:21:21,749
In 2016, scientists studying a high pass called the Col de la Traversette reported a layer of churned-up earth full of horse dung dating to around this time.

207
00:21:22,129 --> 00:21:24,822
It is an intriguing clue, but not proof.

208
00:21:26,822 --> 00:21:33,820
When Hannibal came down into the valley of the Po, he had twenty thousand infantry and six thousand cavalry left.

209
00:21:34,200 --> 00:21:41,442
We know these numbers because he later had them carved on a bronze tablet in southern Italy, where Polybius read them.

210
00:21:41,822 --> 00:21:45,634
He had crossed the Pyrenees with about fifty-nine thousand men.

211
00:21:46,014 --> 00:21:48,045
More than half of them were gone.

212
00:21:48,425 --> 00:21:49,809
But he was in Italy.

213
00:22:00,809 --> 00:22:07,530
The Gallic tribes of northern Italy, who had recently been conquered by Rome, began to come over to Hannibal.

214
00:22:07,910 --> 00:22:13,449
Scipio, back from the Rhône, met him first in a cavalry clash near the river Ticinus.

215
00:22:13,829 --> 00:22:17,537
The Romans were beaten and the consul was badly wounded.

216
00:22:17,917 --> 00:22:27,489
According to the story later told by his friend Laelius, he was saved by his seventeen-year-old son, who charged into the enemy alone to rescue him.

217
00:22:27,869 --> 00:22:31,197
The boy was also named Publius Cornelius Scipio.

218
00:22:31,577 --> 00:22:32,584
Remember him.

219
00:22:34,584 --> 00:22:39,040
In December, at the river Trebia, Hannibal set his first great trap.

220
00:22:39,420 --> 00:22:47,053
Before dawn, on a freezing morning, he sent his Numidian cavalry to ride up to the Roman camp and taunt the enemy.

221
00:22:47,433 --> 00:22:58,826
The second consul, Sempronius, eager for glory, led his army out without breakfast, through the icy river swollen to chest height, and into battle soaked and starving.

222
00:22:59,206 --> 00:23:04,575
Hannibal's men had eaten, rested and rubbed themselves with oil around their fires.

223
00:23:06,575 --> 00:23:15,013
As the battle reached its height, two thousand men under Hannibal's brother Mago burst from a hidden streambed behind the Roman lines.

224
00:23:15,393 --> 00:23:17,082
The Roman army broke.

225
00:23:17,462 --> 00:23:23,316
About ten thousand Romans fought their way through the centre of the enemy line and escaped.

226
00:23:23,696 --> 00:23:28,760
Most of the rest were killed, captured, or drowned trying to recross the river.

227
00:23:30,760 --> 00:23:34,737
That winter almost all of Hannibal's elephants died of the cold.

228
00:23:35,117 --> 00:23:42,316
In the spring of 217 BC he took the army across the flooded marshes of the river Arno.

229
00:23:42,696 --> 00:23:46,211
For four days and three nights the men marched through water.

230
00:23:46,591 --> 00:23:53,422
Many pack animals collapsed and died, and the soldiers slept on their carcasses to keep out of the water.

231
00:23:53,802 --> 00:24:00,244
Hannibal, riding the last surviving elephant, caught an infection that cost him the sight of one eye.

232
00:24:02,244 --> 00:24:05,934
The new consul, Gaius Flaminius, followed him south.

233
00:24:06,314 --> 00:24:12,452
Near Lake Trasimene, the road ran along a narrow strip between the water and a line of hills.

234
00:24:12,832 --> 00:24:16,587
Hannibal hid his entire army on the hills overnight.

235
00:24:16,967 --> 00:24:19,965
In the morning a thick mist rose off the lake.

236
00:24:20,345 --> 00:24:23,988
The Roman column marched into the valley without scouting it.

237
00:24:25,988 --> 00:24:31,032
At a signal, Hannibal's men charged down out of the fog from every direction.

238
00:24:31,412 --> 00:24:35,546
The Romans could not see the enemy, could not form up, could not run.

239
00:24:35,926 --> 00:24:40,522
Livy says the fighting was so fierce that no one noticed an earthquake.

240
00:24:40,902 --> 00:24:43,903
Flaminius was killed by a Gallic horseman.

241
00:24:44,283 --> 00:24:47,895
In three hours, about fifteen thousand Romans died.

242
00:24:48,275 --> 00:24:51,719
Thousands more drowned trying to swim out into the lake.

243
00:24:53,719 --> 00:25:01,529
In Rome, a magistrate stood up before the crowd in the Forum and announced simply: We have been defeated in a great battle.

244
00:25:01,909 --> 00:25:07,882
The Senate turned to an old aristocrat, Quintus Fabius Maximus, and made him dictator.

245
00:25:08,262 --> 00:25:10,061
Fabius had a new idea.

246
00:25:10,441 --> 00:25:12,525
He would not fight Hannibal at all.

247
00:25:14,525 --> 00:25:23,698
He shadowed the Carthaginian army along the hills, cut off its foraging parties and starved it, but refused every invitation to battle.

248
00:25:24,078 --> 00:25:25,734
The Romans hated it.

249
00:25:26,114 --> 00:25:31,211
They called him Cunctator, the Delayer, and laughed at him as Hannibal's servant.

250
00:25:31,591 --> 00:25:35,141
Once, Fabius trapped Hannibal in a valley in Campania.

251
00:25:35,521 --> 00:25:42,450
That night Hannibal had burning torches tied to the horns of two thousand oxen, and drove them up a hillside.

252
00:25:42,830 --> 00:25:49,727
The Romans rushed to block what they thought was the army escaping, and Hannibal slipped through the pass behind them.

253
00:26:00,727 --> 00:26:05,356
By 216 BC, Rome had lost patience with delay.

254
00:26:05,736 --> 00:26:18,923
The voters elected two new consuls, the cautious aristocrat Lucius Aemilius Paullus and the popular Gaius Terentius Varro, and gave them the largest army Rome had ever put into the field.

255
00:26:19,303 --> 00:26:24,547
Eight legions, each one enlarged, with as many again from the Italian allies.

256
00:26:24,927 --> 00:26:30,417
Polybius gives a total of eighty thousand infantry and more than six thousand cavalry.

257
00:26:30,797 --> 00:26:34,014
Their orders were to find Hannibal and destroy him.

258
00:26:36,014 --> 00:26:44,246
They found him in Apulia, in the south-east of Italy, near a small hilltop town called Cannae, beside the river Aufidus.

259
00:26:44,626 --> 00:26:49,113
Hannibal had perhaps forty thousand infantry and ten thousand cavalry.

260
00:26:49,493 --> 00:26:51,834
He was outnumbered almost two to one.

261
00:26:52,214 --> 00:26:58,542
On the second of August, by the Roman calendar, the two armies formed up on the flat ground by the river.

262
00:27:00,542 --> 00:27:05,026
The Romans planned to smash through the centre with sheer weight of numbers.

263
00:27:05,406 --> 00:27:08,448
Hannibal read their plan, and used it against them.

264
00:27:08,828 --> 00:27:17,676
In the centre he placed his Gauls and Spaniards, the least reliable troops, in a line that bulged forward toward the enemy like a crescent.

265
00:27:18,056 --> 00:27:22,368
On either side, held back, stood his best African infantry.

266
00:27:22,748 --> 00:27:24,611
On the wings, his cavalry.

267
00:27:26,611 --> 00:27:28,734
The battle began on the wings.

268
00:27:29,114 --> 00:27:43,013
On the riverside, Hannibal's heavy cavalry under Hasdrubal crushed the Roman horsemen, then rode all the way around behind the Roman army to attack the allied cavalry on the far wing, and sent them fleeing too.

269
00:27:43,393 --> 00:27:47,209
Meanwhile the Roman infantry pushed forward into the centre.

270
00:27:47,589 --> 00:27:53,141
The Gauls and Spaniards gave ground, slowly, step by step, exactly as planned.

271
00:27:53,521 --> 00:27:55,316
The crescent bent backward.

272
00:27:55,696 --> 00:27:58,769
The Romans pressed into it, thinking they were winning.

273
00:28:00,769 --> 00:28:02,773
Then Hannibal sprang the trap.

274
00:28:03,153 --> 00:28:09,278
The African infantry on either side turned inward and attacked the flanks of the Roman mass.

275
00:28:09,658 --> 00:28:14,845
The Carthaginian cavalry came back from the pursuit and fell on the Roman rear.

276
00:28:15,225 --> 00:28:24,942
The Roman army, still pushing forward, found itself surrounded on every side, packed so tightly that many men could not raise their arms to fight.

277
00:28:26,942 --> 00:28:31,895
What followed was not a battle but a slaughter that lasted the rest of the day.

278
00:28:32,275 --> 00:28:35,395
Polybius says seventy thousand Romans died.

279
00:28:35,775 --> 00:28:39,313
Livy gives a lower figure, about forty-eight thousand.

280
00:28:39,693 --> 00:28:44,081
Either way, it was one of the bloodiest single days of fighting in history.

281
00:28:44,460 --> 00:28:46,403
The consul Paullus was killed.

282
00:28:46,783 --> 00:28:49,996
So were two former consuls and some eighty senators.

283
00:28:50,376 --> 00:28:54,220
Hannibal lost about six thousand men, most of them Gauls.

284
00:28:56,220 --> 00:29:11,433
Livy tells us that Hannibal's brother Mago later stood before the council in Carthage and poured out the gold rings taken from the fingers of dead Roman nobles: a heap so large that, by some accounts, it filled three and a half bushels.

285
00:29:11,813 --> 00:29:15,456
More than two thousand years later, armies still study Cannae.

286
00:29:15,836 --> 00:29:22,812
The double envelopment, the perfect encirclement of a larger force, is still often called simply, a Cannae.

287
00:29:33,812 --> 00:29:39,932
After Cannae, Hannibal's cavalry commander Maharbal urged him to march on Rome at once.

288
00:29:40,312 --> 00:29:44,966
Give me the cavalry, he said, and in five days you will dine on the Capitol.

289
00:29:45,346 --> 00:29:46,685
Hannibal refused.

290
00:29:47,065 --> 00:29:49,444
Livy says Maharbal turned on him.

291
00:29:49,824 --> 00:29:52,668
The gods have not given everything to one man.

292
00:29:53,048 --> 00:29:55,341
You know how to win a victory, Hannibal.

293
00:29:55,721 --> 00:29:57,904
You do not know how to use one.

294
00:29:59,904 --> 00:30:02,343
Historians still argue about it.

295
00:30:02,723 --> 00:30:09,840
But Rome was a well-fortified city more than three hundred kilometres away, and Hannibal had no siege equipment.

296
00:30:10,220 --> 00:30:13,104
His strategy had never been to storm Rome.

297
00:30:13,484 --> 00:30:19,957
It was to break up Rome's alliance of Italian cities, one by one, until Rome stood alone.

298
00:30:21,957 --> 00:30:23,758
At first it seemed to work.

299
00:30:24,138 --> 00:30:27,640
Capua, the second city of Italy, went over to him.

300
00:30:28,020 --> 00:30:29,775
So did much of the south.

301
00:30:30,155 --> 00:30:34,329
King Philip the Fifth of Macedon made an alliance with Carthage.

302
00:30:34,709 --> 00:30:38,773
In Sicily, the great Greek city of Syracuse changed sides.

303
00:30:39,154 --> 00:30:41,299
Rome was fighting on four fronts.

304
00:30:43,299 --> 00:30:45,043
But Rome did not break.

305
00:30:45,423 --> 00:30:55,988
In the panic after Cannae, the Romans even buried two Gauls and two Greeks alive in the cattle market, an ancient rite that Livy calls hardly Roman at all.

306
00:30:56,368 --> 00:31:00,491
The Senate refused to ransom the prisoners Hannibal had taken.

307
00:31:00,871 --> 00:31:05,790
It armed eight thousand slaves, and raised new legions from boys and old men.

308
00:31:06,170 --> 00:31:11,555
And the Latin cities of central Italy, the core of Rome's alliance, stayed loyal.

309
00:31:13,555 --> 00:31:20,532
At Syracuse, a Roman army and fleet laid siege to the city in 213 BC.

310
00:31:20,912 --> 00:31:36,107
For two years they were held off by the inventions of one old man, the mathematician Archimedes: catapults that could strike ships at any range, and great cranes with iron claws that lifted Roman galleys out of the water and dropped them.

311
00:31:36,487 --> 00:31:45,945
When the city finally fell in 212, a Roman soldier killed Archimedes, according to the story, while he was drawing in the sand.

312
00:31:47,945 --> 00:31:56,003
In 211, while Roman armies besieged Capua, Hannibal marched on Rome itself, hoping to draw them away.

313
00:31:56,383 --> 00:32:01,355
He camped five kilometres from the city and rode up to the walls with his cavalry.

314
00:32:01,735 --> 00:32:10,053
Livy says that, at that very moment, the land where Hannibal was camped was put up for sale in Rome, and sold at full price.

315
00:32:10,433 --> 00:32:12,734
The Romans did not lift the siege.

316
00:32:13,114 --> 00:32:15,613
Hannibal withdrew, and Capua fell.

317
00:32:17,613 --> 00:32:25,113
Then, in 207 BC, Hannibal's brother Hasdrubal crossed the Alps from Spain with a second army.

318
00:32:25,493 --> 00:32:29,352
If the brothers could join forces, the war might still be won.

319
00:32:29,732 --> 00:32:32,558
But Hasdrubal's messengers were captured.

320
00:32:32,938 --> 00:32:40,892
A Roman consul marched north in secret, joined his colleague, and at the river Metaurus destroyed Hasdrubal's army.

321
00:32:41,272 --> 00:32:43,124
Hasdrubal was killed.

322
00:32:43,504 --> 00:32:48,519
The Romans cut off his head, carried it south, and threw it into Hannibal's camp.

323
00:32:50,519 --> 00:32:56,901
Livy says that when Hannibal recognised his brother's face, he said that he now saw the fate of Carthage.

324
00:32:57,281 --> 00:33:01,495
He withdrew to the mountains of the far south of Italy, to Bruttium.

325
00:33:01,875 --> 00:33:06,169
For four more years he stayed there, undefeated, but going nowhere.

326
00:33:17,169 --> 00:33:19,927
In Spain, the war had gone badly for Rome.

327
00:33:20,307 --> 00:33:29,437
In 211 BC, both Roman commanders there, the brothers Publius and Gnaeus Scipio, were killed within weeks of each other.

328
00:33:29,817 --> 00:33:32,573
Rome needed someone to take their place.

329
00:33:32,953 --> 00:33:41,742
The man who stepped forward was the son who had saved his father's life at the Ticinus seven years before, Publius Cornelius Scipio.

330
00:33:42,122 --> 00:33:45,836
He was about twenty-five, and he had never held high office.

331
00:33:46,216 --> 00:33:48,716
The people gave him the command anyway.

332
00:33:50,716 --> 00:33:53,522
His first move was the boldest of the war.

333
00:33:53,902 --> 00:34:05,497
In 209 BC he marched his army in seven days straight to New Carthage, the enemy's capital in Spain, while the three Carthaginian armies there were far away.

334
00:34:05,877 --> 00:34:09,422
The city stood on a peninsula with a lagoon behind it.

335
00:34:09,802 --> 00:34:15,857
Scipio had learned from fishermen that the lagoon became shallow enough to wade at certain times of day.

336
00:34:16,237 --> 00:34:25,325
While his main army attacked the walls from the front, a detachment waded across the lagoon and climbed the undefended walls on the other side.

337
00:34:27,325 --> 00:34:36,489
New Carthage fell in a single day, with its treasury, its arsenals, its ships, and the hostages Carthage had taken from the Spanish tribes.

338
00:34:36,869 --> 00:34:39,116
Scipio sent the hostages home.

339
00:34:39,496 --> 00:34:49,495
Polybius tells a famous story: his soldiers brought him a beautiful young captive, and he returned her, untouched, to her fiancé, a Spanish prince.

340
00:34:49,875 --> 00:34:56,637
The story was told for centuries as an example of noble restraint, and painted over and over again.

341
00:34:58,637 --> 00:35:07,540
Over the next three years Scipio drove the Carthaginians out of Spain altogether, defeating them at Baecula and at Ilipa.

342
00:35:07,920 --> 00:35:14,117
Then he went home, was elected consul, and argued for a plan the old Fabius thought insane.

343
00:35:14,497 --> 00:35:21,273
Instead of chasing Hannibal around Italy, Rome should invade Africa, and force Carthage to call him home.

344
00:35:23,273 --> 00:35:28,242
In 204 BC, Scipio landed in Africa near Utica.

345
00:35:28,622 --> 00:35:39,350
He found a vital ally there: Masinissa, a young Numidian prince who had fought against Rome in Spain, had been driven from his kingdom, and now changed sides.

346
00:35:39,730 --> 00:35:45,416
His horsemen, who rode without saddle or bridle, were the best light cavalry in the world.

347
00:35:47,416 --> 00:35:51,158
In the spring of 203, Scipio struck by night.

348
00:35:51,538 --> 00:35:58,415
The enemy armies of Carthage and its Numidian allies were camped in huts made of reeds and branches.

349
00:35:58,795 --> 00:36:03,233
His men set them on fire, and cut down the soldiers as they ran out.

350
00:36:03,613 --> 00:36:07,477
Polybius calls it the most dazzling of all Scipio's exploits.

351
00:36:07,857 --> 00:36:11,839
A few weeks later he won a pitched battle on the Great Plains.

352
00:36:13,839 --> 00:36:16,361
Carthage did what Scipio had expected.

353
00:36:16,741 --> 00:36:18,384
It called Hannibal home.

354
00:36:18,764 --> 00:36:25,434
In the autumn of 203 BC, after fifteen years in Italy, he sailed for Africa.

355
00:36:25,814 --> 00:36:33,132
Livy says that few exiles ever left their own country as sadly as Hannibal left the country of his enemies.

356
00:36:44,132 --> 00:36:54,402
In 202 BC, the two greatest generals of their age faced each other on a plain near a town called Zama, south-west of Carthage.

357
00:36:54,782 --> 00:37:01,199
Polybius says that before the battle they met in person, between the armies, each with an interpreter.

358
00:37:01,579 --> 00:37:03,103
Hannibal offered peace.

359
00:37:03,483 --> 00:37:05,088
Scipio said it was too late.

360
00:37:05,468 --> 00:37:11,608
Whether they really spoke, or whether the meeting was invented by later writers, we cannot be sure.

361
00:37:13,608 --> 00:37:17,064
Hannibal opened the battle with his eighty war elephants.

362
00:37:17,444 --> 00:37:18,766
Scipio was ready.

363
00:37:19,146 --> 00:37:25,465
He had arranged his infantry not in a solid line, but in columns with open lanes between them.

364
00:37:25,845 --> 00:37:29,838
As the elephants charged, the Romans blew trumpets and horns.

365
00:37:30,218 --> 00:37:35,999
Many of the terrified animals ran down the lanes and out the back without hurting anyone.

366
00:37:36,379 --> 00:37:40,867
Others turned around and stampeded into Hannibal's own cavalry.

367
00:37:42,867 --> 00:37:53,872
Scipio's cavalry, the Italians under his friend Laelius and the Numidians under Masinissa, drove the Carthaginian horsemen off the field and chased them away.

368
00:37:54,252 --> 00:37:55,964
Then the infantry met.

369
00:37:56,344 --> 00:37:58,953
Hannibal's first two lines were broken.

370
00:37:59,333 --> 00:38:06,407
But his third line, his veterans from Italy, held firm, and for a long time the outcome hung in the balance.

371
00:38:08,407 --> 00:38:13,213
Then Scipio's cavalry returned, and charged into the back of Hannibal's line.

372
00:38:13,593 --> 00:38:15,428
It was Cannae in reverse.

373
00:38:15,808 --> 00:38:21,184
Polybius says twenty thousand Carthaginians were killed, and almost as many captured.

374
00:38:21,564 --> 00:38:24,497
Hannibal escaped with a handful of horsemen.

375
00:38:24,877 --> 00:38:28,365
For the first time in his life, he had lost a great battle.

376
00:38:30,365 --> 00:38:35,501
Back in Carthage, a councillor stood up to argue against accepting Rome's terms.

377
00:38:35,881 --> 00:38:39,291
Hannibal walked over and pulled him down from the platform.

378
00:38:39,671 --> 00:38:51,387
Then he told the astonished councillors that he had left Carthage as a boy of nine, and had come back at forty-five, and that he knew more about war than about the customs of their assembly.

379
00:38:51,767 --> 00:38:53,598
Carthage must make peace.

380
00:38:55,598 --> 00:38:58,769
The terms of 201 BC were harsh.

381
00:38:59,149 --> 00:39:03,952
Carthage would keep its lands in Africa but lose everything overseas.

382
00:39:04,332 --> 00:39:08,746
It had to hand over all its elephants and all but ten of its warships.

383
00:39:09,126 --> 00:39:13,193
It had to pay ten thousand talents of silver over fifty years.

384
00:39:13,573 --> 00:39:18,646
And it could not make war on anyone, even in Africa, without Rome's permission.

385
00:39:19,026 --> 00:39:26,110
Livy says the Romans towed some five hundred Carthaginian ships out to sea, and burned them in sight of the city.

386
00:39:28,110 --> 00:39:33,987
Scipio returned to Rome in triumph, and took the name by which he is still known: Africanus.

387
00:39:34,367 --> 00:39:38,070
Hannibal, against all expectation, stayed in Carthage.

388
00:39:38,450 --> 00:39:40,066
He was not finished yet.

389
00:39:51,066 --> 00:39:58,461
In 196 BC, Hannibal was elected suffete, one of the two chief magistrates of Carthage.

390
00:39:58,841 --> 00:40:04,392
He went after the corrupt officials who had grown rich while the state struggled to pay Rome.

391
00:40:04,772 --> 00:40:11,647
He made the powerful court of judges, whose members had held office for life, subject to election every year.

392
00:40:12,027 --> 00:40:20,544
He found that so much public money was being stolen that, once the theft stopped, the indemnity could be paid without any new taxes.

393
00:40:22,544 --> 00:40:33,240
He made powerful enemies, and they sent word to Rome that Hannibal was plotting with King Antiochus the Third, the ruler of the vast Seleucid empire in the East.

394
00:40:33,620 --> 00:40:35,386
A Roman commission arrived.

395
00:40:35,766 --> 00:40:46,160
In 195 BC, Hannibal slipped out of the city at night, rode to the coast, and sailed for Tyre, the ancient mother city of Carthage.

396
00:40:46,540 --> 00:40:48,648
He would never see Africa again.

397
00:40:50,648 --> 00:40:53,993
Meanwhile, Carthage did something unexpected.

398
00:40:54,373 --> 00:40:55,682
It got rich again.

399
00:40:56,062 --> 00:41:00,787
Freed from the cost of empire and war, its farms and its trade flourished.

400
00:41:01,167 --> 00:41:09,108
In 191 BC it offered to pay off the entire rest of the fifty-year indemnity at once.

401
00:41:09,488 --> 00:41:10,730
Rome refused.

402
00:41:11,110 --> 00:41:13,519
The payments were not really about money.

403
00:41:13,899 --> 00:41:16,293
They were a reminder of who was in charge.

404
00:41:18,293 --> 00:41:31,333
Carthaginian agriculture was so admired that, after the city's fall, the Roman Senate ordered a Carthaginian farming manual, by a writer named Mago, to be translated into Latin.

405
00:41:31,714 --> 00:41:37,043
It was the only book from the libraries of Carthage that Rome considered worth keeping.

406
00:41:37,423 --> 00:41:41,047
And archaeologists have found something even more surprising.

407
00:41:41,427 --> 00:41:54,065
The famous circular naval harbour, in the form described by the ancient writers, seems to have been built in this period, after the war with Hannibal, even though Carthage was allowed only ten warships.

408
00:41:56,065 --> 00:42:06,884
Hannibal served Antiochus as an adviser and admiral, but the king ignored most of his advice and was crushed by the Romans in 190 BC.

409
00:42:07,264 --> 00:42:11,107
The peace treaty demanded that Hannibal be handed over.

410
00:42:11,487 --> 00:42:18,596
He fled again, and found shelter at last with King Prusias of Bithynia, in what is now northern Turkey.

411
00:42:18,976 --> 00:42:28,409
There, according to the Roman writer Nepos, he won a sea battle for his host by having jars full of poisonous snakes thrown onto the enemy's ships.

412
00:42:30,409 --> 00:42:37,381
Around 183 BC, the Romans sent an envoy to demand that Prusias surrender him.

413
00:42:37,761 --> 00:42:40,261
Soldiers surrounded Hannibal's house.

414
00:42:40,641 --> 00:42:43,864
He had long kept poison ready, and now he took it.

415
00:42:44,244 --> 00:42:46,241
Livy gives his last words.

416
00:42:46,621 --> 00:42:53,378
Let us free the Romans from their long anxiety, since they think it too long to wait for the death of an old man.

417
00:42:53,758 --> 00:42:55,298
He was about sixty-four.

418
00:42:57,298 --> 00:43:04,154
In about the same year, Scipio Africanus died too, bitter and out of favour, at his country estate.

419
00:43:04,534 --> 00:43:07,668
It was said that he asked to be buried there, not in Rome.

420
00:43:08,048 --> 00:43:10,960
The two great enemies left the stage together.

421
00:43:11,340 --> 00:43:13,912
But the story of Carthage was not over.

422
00:43:24,912 --> 00:43:31,449
Rome's ally in Africa was the Numidian king Masinissa, who had fought beside Scipio at Zama.

423
00:43:31,829 --> 00:43:33,361
He was a remarkable man.

424
00:43:33,741 --> 00:43:40,697
Polybius met him when he was nearly ninety and says he could still ride all day, and stand in one place for hours.

425
00:43:41,077 --> 00:43:46,304
He had made his nomadic people into farmers, and turned Numidia into a powerful kingdom.

426
00:43:48,304 --> 00:43:56,372
The treaty of 201 BC said Carthage could not go to war, even in Africa, without Rome's permission.

427
00:43:56,752 --> 00:43:59,246
Masinissa used this ruthlessly.

428
00:43:59,626 --> 00:44:03,925
Again and again he seized pieces of Carthaginian territory.

429
00:44:04,305 --> 00:44:07,457
Again and again Carthage complained to Rome.

430
00:44:07,837 --> 00:44:14,670
Roman commissions came, looked, and either sided with Masinissa or went home without deciding anything.

431
00:44:16,670 --> 00:44:25,091
Around 153 BC, one of those commissions included an old Roman senator named Marcus Porcius Cato.

432
00:44:25,471 --> 00:44:29,234
Cato had fought in the war against Hannibal as a young man.

433
00:44:29,614 --> 00:44:34,151
He was famous for his harshness, his thrift and his hatred of luxury.

434
00:44:34,531 --> 00:44:36,547
What he saw at Carthage shocked him.

435
00:44:36,927 --> 00:44:39,424
The city was not ruined and humbled.

436
00:44:39,804 --> 00:44:42,713
It was rich, crowded and full of young men.

437
00:44:44,713 --> 00:44:50,799
Back in Rome, Plutarch tells us, he let some figs fall from the fold of his toga in the Senate.

438
00:44:51,179 --> 00:44:59,512
While the senators admired how large and fresh they were, he told them that the country where they grew was only three days' sail from Rome.

439
00:45:01,512 --> 00:45:08,352
From then on, whatever the subject of the debate, Cato ended every speech in the Senate with the same words.

440
00:45:08,732 --> 00:45:10,629
Carthage must be destroyed.

441
00:45:11,009 --> 00:45:18,518
The famous Latin phrase, Carthago delenda est, is a later summary, but the idea is his.

442
00:45:18,898 --> 00:45:24,951
Another senator, Scipio Nasica, always answered the opposite: Carthage should be spared.

443
00:45:25,331 --> 00:45:29,777
Rome, he argued, needed a rival to fear, to keep it disciplined.

444
00:45:31,777 --> 00:45:37,610
In 151 BC, Carthage made the last of its fifty annual payments.

445
00:45:37,990 --> 00:45:39,856
In its own eyes it was free.

446
00:45:40,236 --> 00:45:47,882
Soon afterwards, after yet another attack by Masinissa, it raised an army of its own and marched out to fight him.

447
00:45:48,262 --> 00:45:52,887
It was beaten, surrounded and starved, and most of the army was killed.

448
00:45:53,267 --> 00:45:59,066
A young Roman officer who happened to be visiting Masinissa watched the battle from a hill.

449
00:45:59,446 --> 00:46:01,884
His name was Scipio Aemilianus.

450
00:46:03,884 --> 00:46:13,887
He was the son of the Roman general Aemilius Paullus, adopted into the family of the Scipios: the grandson, by adoption, of Scipio Africanus.

451
00:46:14,267 --> 00:46:18,875
And the battle he had just watched gave Rome what Cato had been waiting for.

452
00:46:19,255 --> 00:46:22,191
Carthage had made war without Rome's permission.

453
00:46:22,571 --> 00:46:24,105
It had broken the treaty.

454
00:46:35,105 --> 00:46:39,076
In 149 BC, Rome declared war.

455
00:46:39,456 --> 00:46:47,874
Utica, Carthage's oldest neighbour, saw what was coming and went over to Rome, giving the Romans a harbour just up the coast.

456
00:46:48,254 --> 00:46:56,530
The two consuls of the year sailed from Sicily with, according to Appian, eighty thousand infantry and four thousand cavalry.

457
00:46:56,910 --> 00:47:01,537
Carthage had no allies, no fleet, and no army left to speak of.

458
00:47:01,917 --> 00:47:04,063
It did the only thing it could think of.

459
00:47:04,443 --> 00:47:05,450
It surrendered.

460
00:47:07,450 --> 00:47:15,746
Under Roman custom, a people that surrendered gave themselves completely into the hands of Rome, and trusted to its good faith.

461
00:47:16,126 --> 00:47:23,875
The Carthaginian envoys were told that the city would keep its freedom, its laws and its territory, on certain conditions.

462
00:47:24,255 --> 00:47:30,836
The first was that they send three hundred children from their noblest families to Sicily, as hostages.

463
00:47:31,216 --> 00:47:31,970
They did.

464
00:47:32,350 --> 00:47:38,504
Appian describes the mothers clinging to the ships as they sailed, some swimming after them into the sea.

465
00:47:40,504 --> 00:47:44,844
Then the consuls, now camped at Utica, gave the second order.

466
00:47:45,224 --> 00:47:49,399
Carthage did not need weapons, since it was under Rome's protection.

467
00:47:49,779 --> 00:47:51,512
It must hand them all over.

468
00:47:51,892 --> 00:47:53,689
Again, Carthage obeyed.

469
00:47:54,069 --> 00:48:02,195
Appian says the Romans received two hundred thousand sets of armour, countless javelins and arrows, and two thousand catapults.

470
00:48:02,575 --> 00:48:05,723
Long wagon trains carried them out of the city.

471
00:48:07,723 --> 00:48:11,305
Then the consul Censorinus gave the final order.

472
00:48:11,685 --> 00:48:14,522
The people of Carthage must leave their city.

473
00:48:14,902 --> 00:48:17,312
The Romans were going to destroy it.

474
00:48:17,692 --> 00:48:26,331
The Carthaginians could build a new town wherever they liked, provided it was at least eighty stades, about fifteen kilometres, from the sea.

475
00:48:26,712 --> 00:48:32,045
For a city that had lived from the sea for six hundred years, it was a sentence of death.

476
00:48:34,045 --> 00:48:37,469
When the envoys brought the news home, the city went mad.

477
00:48:37,849 --> 00:48:43,099
Crowds dragged the councillors who had advised surrender from their seats and killed them.

478
00:48:43,479 --> 00:48:46,541
Italians who happened to be in the city were murdered.

479
00:48:46,921 --> 00:48:52,724
People ran to the empty arsenals, to the temples, to the ship sheds where there were no ships.

480
00:48:53,104 --> 00:48:56,642
Then, in desperation, Carthage decided to fight.

481
00:48:57,022 --> 00:48:58,531
It closed its gates.

482
00:48:58,911 --> 00:49:01,640
It freed its slaves to serve as soldiers.

483
00:49:02,020 --> 00:49:10,002
And it recalled a general it had condemned to death only months before, Hasdrubal, who still had an army in the countryside.

484
00:49:12,002 --> 00:49:14,410
The city became one great workshop.

485
00:49:14,790 --> 00:49:25,106
Appian says that every day the Carthaginians made one hundred shields, three hundred swords, a thousand catapult bolts, and five hundred spears and javelins.

486
00:49:25,486 --> 00:49:31,349
Temples and public buildings were turned into forges, men and women working day and night.

487
00:49:31,729 --> 00:49:34,149
There was not enough rope for the catapults.

488
00:49:34,529 --> 00:49:40,751
So, Appian says, the women of Carthage cut off their hair and gave it to be twisted into cords.

489
00:49:51,751 --> 00:49:54,972
The Romans had expected to walk into an empty city.

490
00:49:55,352 --> 00:49:59,873
Instead they faced the strongest fortifications in the Mediterranean.

491
00:50:00,253 --> 00:50:09,646
On the landward side, across the neck of the peninsula, Appian describes a triple line of defences, with a main wall thirteen metres high.

492
00:50:10,026 --> 00:50:17,607
Inside the wall were stables for three hundred elephants and four thousand horses, and barracks for twenty thousand infantry.

493
00:50:19,607 --> 00:50:21,292
The first assaults failed.

494
00:50:21,672 --> 00:50:31,159
The consuls brought up two enormous battering rams and broke a section of the wall, but the Carthaginians drove them back and rebuilt it in the night.

495
00:50:31,539 --> 00:50:34,175
They sent fire ships into the Roman fleet.

496
00:50:34,555 --> 00:50:38,259
Disease spread through the Roman camp beside a marshy lake.

497
00:50:38,639 --> 00:50:41,531
For two years, the war went badly for Rome.

498
00:50:43,531 --> 00:50:49,998
One officer kept distinguishing himself: the young military tribune Scipio Aemilianus.

499
00:50:50,378 --> 00:50:52,587
When others blundered, he saved them.

500
00:50:52,967 --> 00:51:01,277
Even Cato, in his last months of life, quoted Homer about him: he alone has understanding; the rest are flitting shadows.

501
00:51:01,657 --> 00:51:11,192
In 148 BC old Masinissa died, at around ninety, and Scipio was given the task of dividing his kingdom among his sons.

502
00:51:13,192 --> 00:51:24,687
In 147 BC, the Roman people elected Scipio Aemilianus consul, even though he was too young for the office and was only standing for a lower post.

503
00:51:25,067 --> 00:51:26,669
The law was set aside.

504
00:51:27,049 --> 00:51:36,980
He took command at Carthage, and with him, as his adviser and friend, came the Greek historian Polybius, who would see everything that followed with his own eyes.

505
00:51:38,980 --> 00:51:40,638
Scipio cut Carthage off.

506
00:51:41,018 --> 00:51:48,033
He built a fortified camp across the whole width of the isthmus, so that nothing could reach the city by land.

507
00:51:48,413 --> 00:51:56,507
Then he began to build a great stone mole out into the sea, to block the entrance of the harbours, so that no supply ship could get in.

508
00:51:58,507 --> 00:52:01,774
The Carthaginians answered with a feat of their own.

509
00:52:02,154 --> 00:52:07,837
Working in secret, they dug a new channel from the inner harbour straight out to the open sea.

510
00:52:08,217 --> 00:52:13,537
At the same time, from old timber, they built a new fleet of some fifty warships.

511
00:52:13,917 --> 00:52:21,638
One morning, the Romans watched in amazement as a Carthaginian fleet sailed out of a harbour that they thought was sealed.

512
00:52:23,638 --> 00:52:25,629
But the surprise was wasted.

513
00:52:26,009 --> 00:52:33,620
Appian says that instead of attacking at once, the Carthaginian fleet sailed out, showed itself, and sailed back in.

514
00:52:34,000 --> 00:52:45,476
When it finally fought, three days later, the battle was indecisive, and on the way back into the harbour the Carthaginian ships jammed at the narrow entrance and were badly mauled.

515
00:52:47,476 --> 00:52:50,958
Inside the city, Hasdrubal had taken control.

516
00:52:51,338 --> 00:53:01,846
To make sure no one in Carthage could hope for mercy, he had the Roman prisoners he held brought up onto the walls, in full view of their comrades, and tortured to death.

517
00:53:02,226 --> 00:53:04,171
Now there could be no turning back.

518
00:53:04,551 --> 00:53:11,968
Through the winter of 147, the Romans destroyed the last Carthaginian army in the countryside.

519
00:53:12,348 --> 00:53:14,802
In the city, people began to starve.

520
00:53:25,802 --> 00:53:30,026
In the spring of 146 BC, Scipio attacked.

521
00:53:30,406 --> 00:53:32,552
His men broke in by the harbours.

522
00:53:32,932 --> 00:53:43,632
Scipio's friend Gaius Laelius took the wall around the circular naval harbour, and the Romans poured into the great public square of Carthage, its agora, near the sea.

523
00:53:44,012 --> 00:53:47,171
Starving and exhausted, the defenders fell back.

524
00:53:47,551 --> 00:53:56,842
That night, Roman soldiers stripped the gold from a temple of Apollo, as the Greeks called the Phoenician god Reshef, hacking it off with their swords.

525
00:53:58,842 --> 00:54:05,635
From the agora, three narrow streets climbed the hill to the Byrsa, the citadel at the heart of the city.

526
00:54:06,015 --> 00:54:08,799
They were lined with houses six storeys high.

527
00:54:09,179 --> 00:54:12,397
This is where the fighting of our opening scene took place.

528
00:54:12,777 --> 00:54:20,508
For six days and nights, the Romans fought their way up, house by house, crossing from roof to roof on planks and beams.

529
00:54:20,888 --> 00:54:25,739
Then, to clear a path for the troops, Scipio ordered the streets set on fire.

530
00:54:27,739 --> 00:54:49,221
The excavations on the Byrsa hill in the 1970s and 1980s, led by the French archaeologist Serge Lancel, found exactly this: a district of tall houses built in the second century BC, ending in a thick layer of ash, burnt timber, fallen roof tiles and sling stones.

531
00:54:51,221 --> 00:54:58,850
On the seventh day, the people on the Byrsa sent envoys down to ask that those who came out would be allowed to live.

532
00:54:59,230 --> 00:55:01,895
Scipio agreed, except for deserters.

533
00:55:02,275 --> 00:55:05,917
Fifty thousand men and women came out of the citadel.

534
00:55:06,297 --> 00:55:16,559
Nine hundred Roman deserters, who knew they would be shown no mercy, took refuge in the temple of Eshmun, the god of healing, at the very summit of the hill.

535
00:55:16,939 --> 00:55:20,529
With them were Hasdrubal, his wife and their two sons.

536
00:55:22,529 --> 00:55:24,338
Then Hasdrubal broke.

537
00:55:24,718 --> 00:55:29,688
He slipped out alone, carrying an olive branch, and threw himself at Scipio's feet.

538
00:55:30,068 --> 00:55:33,876
Scipio had him displayed to the deserters on the temple roof.

539
00:55:34,256 --> 00:55:41,524
And on the roof, in front of everyone, Hasdrubal's wife appeared, in her finest clothes, holding her two sons.

540
00:55:41,904 --> 00:55:48,075
Polybius, who was there, records that she called out to her husband as a traitor and a coward.

541
00:55:48,455 --> 00:55:54,091
Then she killed her children, threw them into the burning temple, and followed them into the flames.

542
00:55:56,091 --> 00:55:59,909
Later writers said Carthage burned for seventeen days.

543
00:56:00,289 --> 00:56:03,041
As Scipio watched, Polybius saw him weep.

544
00:56:03,421 --> 00:56:11,808
He was thinking, he said, of how cities and empires rise and fall, as Troy had fallen, and Assyria, and Persia, and Macedon.

545
00:56:12,188 --> 00:56:18,275
Then he quoted the lines of Homer in which a Trojan hero foresees the fall of his own city.

546
00:56:18,655 --> 00:56:22,506
When Polybius asked what he meant, Scipio took him by the hand.

547
00:56:22,886 --> 00:56:27,265
He feared, he said, that one day someone would say the same about Rome.

548
00:56:38,265 --> 00:56:42,124
The Senate sent ten commissioners to decide what should be done.

549
00:56:42,504 --> 00:56:48,580
Whatever was left of Carthage was to be razed to the ground, and a curse was laid upon the site.

550
00:56:48,960 --> 00:56:51,519
No one was ever to live there again.

551
00:56:51,899 --> 00:56:55,240
The libraries were given away to the Numidian kings.

552
00:56:55,620 --> 00:57:00,058
Its territory became a new Roman province, called simply Africa.

553
00:57:00,438 --> 00:57:15,936
In the same year, on the other side of the Mediterranean, another Roman army sacked the great Greek city of Corinth. 146 BC was the year Rome became the unchallenged master of the Mediterranean world.

554
00:57:17,936 --> 00:57:24,758
You may have heard that the Romans ploughed the ground and sowed it with salt, so that nothing would ever grow there again.

555
00:57:25,138 --> 00:57:27,119
There is no ancient source for this.

556
00:57:27,499 --> 00:57:31,535
The story first appears in modern books, in the twentieth century.

557
00:57:31,915 --> 00:57:33,026
It is a myth.

558
00:57:35,026 --> 00:57:36,875
The curse did not last long.

559
00:57:37,255 --> 00:57:43,401
A generation later, the reformer Gaius Gracchus tried to found a Roman colony on the site.

560
00:57:43,781 --> 00:57:47,796
Julius Caesar planned another, and his heir Augustus built it.

561
00:57:48,176 --> 00:58:01,698
Roman Carthage became one of the greatest cities of the empire, the capital of Africa, with vast public baths, a circus, an amphitheatre and theatres, its harbours rebuilt on the old Punic plan.

562
00:58:02,078 --> 00:58:07,108
It was in Roman Carthage, centuries later, that the young Augustine studied.

563
00:58:09,108 --> 00:58:12,239
The Phoenician language, Punic, lived on too.

564
00:58:12,618 --> 00:58:19,841
It was still spoken in the countryside of North Africa six hundred years after the fall, in the time of Augustine.

565
00:58:20,221 --> 00:58:33,619
Carthage itself passed to the Vandals, then the Byzantines, and was finally abandoned after the Arab conquest at the end of the seventh century, its stones carried off for centuries to build Tunis and other cities.

566
00:58:35,619 --> 00:58:42,672
Today the site of Carthage is a quiet suburb of Tunis, with villas, gardens and a presidential palace.

567
00:58:43,052 --> 00:58:47,757
Since 1979 it has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site.

568
00:58:48,137 --> 00:58:55,943
You can still walk around the circular harbour, now a lagoon, with its small round island where the admiral's tower once stood.

569
00:58:56,323 --> 00:59:03,270
On the Byrsa hill, the houses that burned in 146 BC have been uncovered again.

570
00:59:03,650 --> 00:59:08,269
And in the tophet, the little stones of Tanit still stand in the grass.

571
00:59:10,269 --> 00:59:23,283
We know Carthage almost entirely through the eyes of its enemies: Polybius, the friend of the man who destroyed it; Livy, who wrote Rome's national epic; and Appian, two centuries later still.

572
00:59:23,663 --> 00:59:25,544
Carthage's own books are gone.

573
00:59:25,924 --> 00:59:28,518
Its own version of its story is lost.

574
00:59:28,898 --> 00:59:30,661
We will never hear its side.

575
00:59:32,661 --> 00:59:38,923
Cato died in 149 BC, before he could see the war he had demanded won.

576
00:59:39,303 --> 00:59:51,917
Scipio Aemilianus, who had wept for Rome as he burned Carthage, was found dead in his bed twenty years later, during the political violence that would eventually tear the Roman Republic apart.

577
00:59:52,297 --> 00:59:58,295
And Hannibal, who never captured Rome, became more famous than almost any Roman of his age.

578
00:59:58,675 --> 01:00:01,684
Generals have studied his campaigns ever since.

579
01:00:03,684 --> 01:00:09,222
For generations, Romans used the words Hannibal at the gates to mean mortal danger.

580
01:00:09,602 --> 01:00:16,986
They never forgot that, for fifteen years, one man from a city they destroyed had held Rome itself in the balance.

581
01:00:17,366 --> 01:00:19,375
Carthage had been the New City.

582
01:00:19,755 --> 01:00:23,565
Rome made sure it would also be the last city of its kind.

583
01:00:23,945 --> 01:00:26,386
And Scipio's fear, at least, came true.

584
01:00:26,766 --> 01:00:32,490
Six centuries later, Rome itself would be sacked, and its empire in the west would fall.

